    "teacher_markdown": "### **Teacher Tips**\r\n\r\n**Students may think the bug is in the button instead of the function**\r\n\r\nStudents may notice that both buttons produce the same result and assume the event listeners are broken. Help them trace the flow by asking: **\"What function do both buttons call?\"** This can help students recognize that the problem is in the shared function, not the buttons themselves.\r\n\r\nRelevant code:\r\n```javascript\r\nblueTeeBtn.addEventListener(\"click\", () => {\r\n  addShirt();\r\n});\r\n\r\nredTeeBtn.addEventListener(\"click\", () => {\r\n  addShirt();\r\n});\r\n```\r\n\r\nBoth buttons call the same function with no information about which shirt was clicked.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n**Students may overlook the value of parameters**\r\n\r\nSome students may try to create separate functions for each shirt rather than updating the existing function to accept information. Encourage them to think about inputs by asking: **\"How can one function know which shirt was clicked?\"**\r\n\r\nRelevant code:\r\n```javascript\r\nconst addShirt = (shirtName, sizeSelect) => {\r\n  const selectedSize = sizeSelect.value;\r\n  display.textContent = selectedSize + \" \" + shirtName;\r\n};\r\n```\r\n\r\nThis helps students see how parameters make a function more flexible.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n**Students may ignore the debugging information**\r\n\r\nWhen students open the debug panel, they may see the console output but not know how it relates to the bug. Encourage them to use the information as evidence by asking: **\"What does the console say when you click the blue shirt versus the red shirt?\"** Remind students that debugging tools help them understand what the program thinks is happening.\r\n\r\nRelevant code:\r\n```javascript\r\nconsole.log(\"Blue Shirt clicked!\");\r\n```\r\n\r\nUpdated version:\r\n```javascript\r\nconsole.log(shirtName + \" clicked!\");\r\n```\r\n\r\nThis can help students verify that the correct information is being passed into the function."
  },
